(project borge-ui
 (doc "Declarative UI DSL — an extension of the borge language, not a separate one.
       Adds (theme), (component), (layout), (variant), (page), (routes) and related
       forms inside .borg sections. All existing borge commands work on UI sections.
       The spec is the artifact — borge does NOT generate code. Renderer-agnostic
       semantics for human interpretation and implementation.")
 (no-inline)
 (status planned)
 (exports parse_ui validate_ui ui_to_json
          parse_component parse_layout parse_page)

 (section relationship-to-borge
  (doc "UI is an extension of the borge language, not a separate language")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Borge UI is NOT a new language. It is a set of new sexp forms
     that live inside existing .borg files. Every borge command that
     works today works on UI sections without changes:

     - borge balance: paren-checks UI sections like any other
     - borge parse: parses UI forms as sexp like anything else
     - borge fmt: formats UI sections with canonical indentation
     - borge check: validates UI section files exist and are well-formed
     - borge lint: validates UI semantics (and gains UI-aware checks)
     - borge report: counts UI section statuses like any other section
     - borge drift: detects drift between UI spec and generated code
     - borge nodes: dumps UI AST like any other section
     - borge make (with appropriate prompt): agent edits UI spec interactively

     The new forms are just sexp — the parser already handles them.
     A (ui ...) section is another section body, same as (doc ...)
     or (details ...). Balance, formatting, and reporting all work
     on day one with zero code changes to the existing tools.

     What IS new:
       1. New sexp forms: (theme), (component), (layout), (variant),
          (page), (routes), (use), (fill), (use-layout), (slot)
       2. NO code generation — the spec IS the UI definition
       3. Future: semantic awareness — lint validates that (use button)
          refers to a defined component, drift checks generated UI code,
          etc. But this is incremental enhancement, not a prerequisite.

     The principle: borge UI sections are first-class borge citizens.
     They get the same tooling, the same workflow, and the same
     spec-is-source-of-truth discipline as every other section.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section philosophy
  (doc (|
    Borge UI is a declarative language for describing user interfaces.
    It borrows Clay's insight: UI layout is just nested boxes with
    flexbox-like rules. But where Clay is a C layout library, borge
    UI is a spec — it describes *what* the interface should look like,
    not *how* to render it.

    The five core principles:

    1. Describe intent, not pixels. Sizing defaults to fractional
       parts (flex portions), not absolute units. You say "sidebar
       gets 1 part, content gets 3 parts" and the generator handles
       the rest.

    2. Renderer-agnostic. The same spec can generate HTML+CSS for
       a Dream web app, React components, Clay C code, or any other
       target. The convention system determines the output format.

    3. Shared base, named variants. Responsive design is handled
       through named variants that overlay the base properties. You
       write shared style once, then describe only what changes at
       different sizes.

    4. Theme variables as the foundation. Colors, spacing, font sizes,
       and corner radii are defined once as theme variables and
       referenced everywhere. Change the theme, change the whole app.
       No hardcoded values scattered across elements.

    5. Components, layouts, and pages are separate concerns.
       Components are small reusable pieces (buttons, cards, inputs).
       Layouts are page-level compositions with slots (nav bar,
       sidebar, content area). Pages fill layout slots with
       specific content. You don't use a layout the same way you
       use a component — they're different granularity.

    Interactivity is a capability declaration, not a handler binding.
    (interactive click) means "this element is clickable" — the
    target generator decides how to implement that. The spec does
    not couple to any host language's function naming or event system.
  |))
 )

 (section theme
  (doc "Theme variables — shared values referenced by all UI elements")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     The (theme ...) form defines named variables for colors, spacing,
     font sizes, and corner radii. Every UI element references these
     variables instead of hardcoding values. Change the theme, change
     the whole app.

     (theme
       (palette bg-primary "#e0d7d2")
       (palette bg-surface "#f5f0eb")
       (palette text-primary "#1a1a1a")
       (palette text-muted "#666666")
       (palette accent "#a8421c")
       (spacing xs 4)
       (spacing sm 8)
       (spacing md 16)
       (spacing lg 24)
       (spacing xl 32)
       (font-size sm 12)
       (font-size base 14)
       (font-size lg 18)
       (font-size xl 24)
       (radius sm 4)
       (radius md 8)
       (radius lg 12))

     Referencing palette variables in elements:

       (bg (palette bg-primary))
       (color (palette text-primary))

     Referencing spacing, font-size, radius variables:

       (padding (spacing md))
       (text "Hello" (size (font-size lg)))
       (corner (radius md))

     The generator resolves variables to literal values per target.
     CSS: var(--bg-primary) or inline. Clay: hex literal.
     React: theme context or CSS variable.

     Two-phase color workflow:

     1. Sketch phase: use keyword colors like (bg blue),
        (color white), (bg dark). The LLM picks actual hex
        values when generating code. Fast for prototyping.
     2. Refinement phase: define a (palette ...) and reference
        it like (bg (palette primary)). Precise, unified,
        consistent across the whole app.

     Keyword colors and palette references can coexist. The spec
     doesn't force precision before you're ready. A work-in-progress
     might have (bg blue) on some elements and (bg (palette primary))
     on others. The palette wins during refinement.

     Recognized keyword colors: red, orange, yellow, green, blue,
     purple, pink, white, black, gray, dark, light, transparent.
     The generator interprets these per target and convention.

     Theme variables are flat: (palette name value), (spacing name value),
     (font-size name value), (radius name value). No nesting, no
     grouping beyond the category prefix. This keeps the theme
     simple to read and simple to generate from.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section ui-form
  (doc "The (ui ...) form — the core building block")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     A ui element is declared with the (ui name ...) form. Name is
     a symbol identifying the element. The body contains property
     declarations and children.

     (ui sidebar
       (layout vertical)
       (width 1)
       (height grow)
       (padding (spacing md))
       (gap (spacing sm))
       (bg (palette bg-primary))
       (children
         (ui header ...)))

     Name is optional for anonymous elements:
       (ui (text "Status") (size (font-size sm)))

     Properties can appear in any order. Children are declared
     explicitly with (children ...). This keeps the structure
     unambiguous — properties and children are never mixed.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section layout-properties
  (doc "How elements are arranged inside their parent")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     (layout direction)
       Direction: horizontal (default) or vertical.
       Children are laid out along the chosen axis.

     (layout horizontal)   — left to right
     (layout vertical)     — top to bottom
   |))
  )

  (subsection sizing
   (doc "How elements size themselves within their parent")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      (width N)        — N fractional parts of parent (flex: N)
      (height N)       — same, for height

      (width grow)     — fill remaining space after siblings are sized
      (height grow)    — same, for height

      (width full)    — 100% of parent
      (height full)   — same, for height

      (width auto)     — size to content
      (height auto)   — size to content

      Explicit units when needed:
      (width (px 300))   — 300 pixels
      (width (rem 2))    — 2rem
      (width (pct 25))   — 25%
      (height (vh 80))   — 80% of viewport height

      Fractional parts are the default and preferred. A sidebar
      getting (width 1) and content getting (width 3) produces
      a 25/75 split without any pixel math. The generator
      translates "1 part" to whatever the target supports
      (flex-grow in CSS, SIZING_GROW in Clay, etc.).

      The minimum: two numbers. If sidebar is 1 and content is 3,
      that's a 1:3 ratio. You don't need to think about pixels
      unless you specifically need them.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection spacing
   (doc "Padding, gaps, and alignment between children")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      (padding N)          — equal padding on all sides
      (padding (top N) (right N) (bottom N) (left N))  — per-side

      (gap N)              — space between child elements

      (align-x left)      — horizontal alignment of children
      (align-x center)
      (align-x right)

      (align-y top)       — vertical alignment of children
      (align-y center)
      (align-y bottom)

      Padding and gap values accept the same unit types as sizing:
      bare numbers are px (since fractional padding is rare),
      or use (rem 1), (pct 5), etc. Theme variable references
      also work: (padding (spacing md)), (gap (spacing sm)).
    |))
   )
  )
 )

 (section style-properties
  (doc "Visual appearance — colors, borders, corners")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     (bg COLOR)            — background color
     (color COLOR)         — text color (inherits to children)
     (border (width N) (color COLOR))  — border on all sides
     (border (bottom (width N) (color COLOR)))  — specific side
     (corner N)            — uniform corner radius in px
     (corner (top-left N) (top-right N) (bottom-left N) (bottom-right N))

     Color values accept three forms:
       - Hex: "#a8421c" or "#a8421c99" (with alpha)
       - Keyword: blue, red, green, dark, white, etc.
         For sketching — the LLM picks actual values when generating.
       - Palette reference: (palette name)
         For refined specs — references a (palette ...) theme variable.

     The two-phase workflow: sketch with keywords, refine with palette.
     (bg blue) during prototyping, (bg (palette primary)) later.

     For opacity: "#a8421c99" (hex with alpha) or
     (bg "#a8421c" (alpha 0.6)).
   |))
  )
 )

 (section text
  (doc "Text elements")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     (text STRING)           — text content
     (text STRING (size N))  — with font size
     (text STRING (size N) (color COLOR))
     (text STRING (bold))
     (placeholder STRING)    — placeholder text for inputs

     Text inherits color from parent unless overridden.
     Font size in px by default, or (size (rem 1.5)),
     or (size (font-size base)) for theme variable.
     (bold) and (italic) are boolean flags.

     Text wrapping: (wrap words) is default — text wraps at
     word boundaries. (wrap none) disables wrapping.
     (wrap newlines) wraps only on newline characters.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section interactive
  (doc "Capability declarations — what the user can do to an element")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     (interactive click)   — this element responds to clicks/taps
     (interactive type)    — this element accepts text input
     (interactive hover)   — this element responds to hover/focus

     Interactive is optional. Omit it for non-interactive elements
     (labels, spacers, containers). When present, it tells the
     generator: "make this element respond to this kind of input."

     The spec does NOT describe handler implementation — no
     function names, no callback wiring. That's code, not spec.
     The generator adds the right event bindings for the target:
       - HTML: an onclick attribute, event listener, or htmx trigger
       - React: an onClick prop
       - Clay: an OnHover callback

     Example:
       (ui save-button
         (interactive click)
         (text "Save")
         (bg (palette accent))
         (padding (top (spacing sm)) (right (spacing md))
                  (bottom (spacing sm)) (left (spacing md)))
         (corner (radius sm)))
   |))
  )
 )

 (section scroll
  (doc "Scrollable containers")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     (scroll vertical)    — scroll content vertically
     (scroll horizontal)  — scroll content horizontally
     (scroll both)        — scroll in both directions
   |))
  )
 )

 (section floating
  (doc "Elements that float above the normal layout flow")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     (float)               — float above siblings, absolute positioned
     (float (attach parent)) — position relative to parent
     (float (offset (top 8))) — offset from attachment point

     Floating elements don't affect their parent's size or
     sibling positioning. They stack on top. Use for tooltips,
     modals, dropdowns.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section image
  (doc "Image elements")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     (image NAME)          — display an image by name/reference
     (image NAME (width N) (height N))  — with explicit size

     The image name is a logical reference. The generator maps
     it to the target's asset system (file path, URL, etc.).
   |))
  )
 )

 (section variants
  (doc "Named variants for responsive design")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     An element can have named variants. Each variant overlays
     the base properties — you only specify what changes.

     (ui sidebar
       (padding (spacing md))
       (gap (spacing sm))
       (bg (palette bg-primary))
       (children ...)

       (variant wide
         (layout vertical)
         (width 1)
         (height grow))

       (variant narrow
         (layout horizontal)
         (width full)
         (height auto)))

     Shared properties (padding, gap, bg, children) are written
     once. Each variant only contains the properties that differ
     from the base.

     Variant names are semantic: wide, narrow, compact, expanded,
     etc. The generator decides what breakpoints or conditions
     map to each variant. The spec says "there are two shapes";
     the convention says "wide = >768px, narrow = <=768px".

     Properties in a variant override the base. Properties not
     listed in a variant are inherited from the base unchanged.

     Children declared in a variant replace the base children.
     If children don't change between variants, declare them
     in the base and omit from variants.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section children
  (doc "Declaring child elements")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Children are declared explicitly with (children ...):

     (ui card
       (layout vertical)
       (padding (spacing md))
       (bg (palette bg-surface))
       (corner (radius lg))
       (children
         (text "Card Title" (size (font-size lg)) (bold))
         (text "Card body text goes here." (size 14))
         (ui action-row
           (layout horizontal)
           (gap (spacing sm))
           (children
             (ui btn (interactive click) (text "OK") (bg (palette accent)))
             (ui btn (interactive click) (text "Cancel") (bg "#999"))))))

     Children are ordered: the order in (children ...) is the
     order on screen, following the layout direction.

     An element with no (children ...) has no children.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section components
  (doc "Reusable UI pieces — defined once, used anywhere")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     A component is a reusable UI element defined with the
     (component name ...) form. Components encapsulate structure
     and style. They're the small pieces — buttons, cards,
     form fields — that get composed into layouts and pages.

     Components have slots: places where content goes when
     the component is used.

     Defining a component:

       (component button
         (interactive click)
         (padding (top (spacing sm)) (right (spacing md))
                  (bottom (spacing sm)) (left (spacing md)))
         (corner (radius sm))
         (align-x center)
         (align-y center)
         (variant primary
           (bg (palette accent)) (color "#ffffff"))
         (variant secondary
           (bg (palette bg-surface)) (color (palette text-primary))))

       (component card
         (layout vertical)
         (padding (spacing md))
         (bg (palette bg-surface))
         (corner (radius lg))
         (gap (spacing sm))
         (children ..content))
         ;; ^^ default slot: children go here when used

     Using a component with (use ...):

       (ui save-btn (use button (variant primary)) (text "Save"))
       (ui info-card (use card)
         (children
           (text "Welcome" (size (font-size lg)))
           (text "Details go here." (size 14))))

     The (use component-name) form instantiates a component.
     Properties after (use ...) override component defaults.
     (variant name) selects which variant to apply.

     Slots:
       ..content  — the default slot. Children of the using
                    element fill this slot.
       (slot name) — named slot. Filled with (fill name ...).

     Named slots let a component define multiple insertion points.
     For example, a card with a header slot and a body slot:

       (component card-with-header
         (layout vertical)
         (padding (spacing md))
         (bg (palette bg-surface))
         (corner (radius lg))
         (children
           (slot header)
           (ui divider (height 1) (bg "#ddd"))
           ..content))

     Filling named slots when using the component:

       (ui my-card (use card-with-header)
         (fill header
           (text "Card Title" (size (font-size lg)) (bold)))
         (children
           (text "Body text goes here.")))

     Components are NOT layouts. A component is a small reusable
     piece. A layout is a page-level composition. You don't drop
     a layout into a card, and you don't slot a button into a
     page shell. Different granularity, different intent.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section layouts
  (doc "Page-level compositions with slots — structural templates for pages")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     A layout is a structural template that defines the shape of a
     page. It uses slots where page-specific content goes. Layouts
     are composed from components but are conceptually different:
     they're structural, not decorative.

     Defining a layout:

       (layout app-shell
         (ui root
           (layout vertical)
           (width full)
           (height full)
           (bg (palette bg-primary))
           (children
             (ui nav-bar
               (layout horizontal)
               (width full)
               (height auto)
               (bg (palette accent))
               (padding (spacing sm))
               (align-y center)
               (gap (spacing md))
               (children
                 (text "App Name" (size (font-size lg)) (color "#ffffff"))
                 (ui spacer (width grow) (height 1))
                 (slot nav-content)))
             (ui body
               (layout horizontal)
               (width full)
               (height grow)
               (children
                 ..content)))))

     (slot name) declares a named slot. (fill name ...) fills it.
     ..content is the default slot — children of the using page
     or filling element go here.

     Layouts often use components inside them:

       (layout sidebar-shell
         (ui root
           (layout vertical)
           (width full)
           (height full)
           (children
             (ui top-bar
               (layout horizontal)
               (width full)
               (height auto)
               (padding (spacing sm))
               (bg (palette accent))
               (children ..nav))
             (ui body
               (layout horizontal)
               (width full)
               (height grow)
               (gap (spacing md))
               (children
                 (ui sidebar
                   (layout vertical)
                   (width 1)
                   (height grow)
                   (bg (palette bg-surface))
                   (corner (radius md))
                   (padding (spacing md))
                   (children ..sidebar))
                 (ui main
                   (layout vertical)
                   (width 3)
                   (height grow)
                   (scroll vertical)
                   (children ..content)))))))

     Layouts are NOT components. A layout defines page structure.
     A component defines a reusable piece. You compose layouts
     from components, but you don't compose components from
     layouts. They're separate because they serve different
     purposes and are used at different scales.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section pages
  (doc "Concrete pages — fill layout slots with specific content")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     A page is a concrete screen in the application. It picks a
     layout and fills its slots with specific content.

     (page home
       (use-layout app-shell)
       (fill nav-content
         (ui nav-link (interactive click) (text "Home"))
         (ui nav-link (interactive click) (text "About")))
       (fill content
         (ui hero
           (layout vertical)
           (padding (spacing xl))
           (align-x center)
           (gap (spacing lg))
           (children
             (text "Welcome" (size (font-size xl)))
             (text "Build something great." (size (font-size base)))))))

     (page dashboard
       (use-layout sidebar-shell)
       (fill nav
         (use button (variant secondary)) (text "Dashboard")
         (use button (variant secondary)) (text "Settings"))
       (fill sidebar
         (use card) (children (text "Nav A"))
         (use card) (children (text "Nav B")))
       (fill content
         (use card) (children (text "Widget 1"))
         (use card) (children (text "Widget 2"))))

     (use-layout name) selects which layout the page uses.
     (fill slot-name ...) provides content for a named slot.
     Unfilled slots are empty — the generator produces
     an empty container in their place.

     Pages can also override layout properties for per-page
     customization. For example, a page might change the
     nav bar background:

       (page special
         (use-layout app-shell
           (override nav-bar (bg "#333")))
         ...)

     But prefer keeping layout overrides minimal. If multiple
     pages need the same override, make a new layout variant
     instead.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section routes
  (doc "URL-to-page mapping for multi-page applications")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     The (routes ...) form maps URLs to pages. The generator
     uses this to produce the routing layer per target.

     (routes
       (route "/" home)
       (route "/dashboard" dashboard)
       (route "/settings" settings))

     Target-specific generation:
       - OCaml/Dream: route handler functions
       - React: router config (React Router, Next.js)
       - Clay: page switch function

     Routes are flat: no nesting, no parameters (yet). Each route
     maps a path string to a page name. The page must be defined
     in the same spec.

     Future: route parameters (/user/:id), nested routes,
     route guards (auth), and redirects.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section app-structure
  (doc "How a complete multi-page UI spec is organized")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     A complete application spec has one top-level (ui app-name ...)
     form containing theme, components, layouts, pages, and routes:

     (ui my-app
       (theme
         (palette bg-primary "#e0d7d2")
         (palette bg-surface "#f5f0eb")
         (palette text-primary "#1a1a1a")
         (palette accent "#a8421c")
         (spacing sm 8) (spacing md 16) (spacing lg 24)
         (font-size sm 12) (font-size base 14) (font-size lg 18)
         (radius sm 4) (radius md 8))

       (component button
         (interactive click)
         (padding (top (spacing sm)) (right (spacing md))
                  (bottom (spacing sm)) (left (spacing md)))
         (corner (radius sm))
         (variant primary (bg (palette accent)) (color "#ffffff"))
         (variant secondary (bg (palette bg-surface)) (color (palette text-primary))))

       (component card
         (layout vertical)
         (padding (spacing md))
         (bg (palette bg-surface))
         (corner (radius lg))
         (gap (spacing sm))
         (children ..content))

       (layout app-shell
         ...)

       (layout sidebar-shell
         ...)

       (page home (use-layout app-shell) ...)
       (page dashboard (use-layout sidebar-shell) ...)

       (routes
         (route "/" home)
         (route "/dashboard" dashboard)))

     The order matters: theme first (everything references it),
     then components (layouts use them), then layouts (pages use
     them), then pages (routes reference them), then routes.

     This is the full stack. A small spec might only have
     (ui name ...) with inline elements and no components or
     layouts. A large app has all five layers. The DSL scales
     from prototype to production.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section full-example
  (doc "A complete example: multi-page text editor")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     (ui text-editor
       (theme
         (palette bg-primary "#e0d7d2")
         (palette bg-surface "#f5f0eb")
         (palette bg-dark "#2a2520")
         (palette text-primary "#1a1a1a")
         (palette text-muted "#888888")
         (palette accent "#a8421c")
         (spacing xs 4) (spacing sm 8) (spacing md 16)
         (spacing lg 24) (spacing xl 32)
         (font-size sm 12) (font-size base 14)
         (font-size lg 18) (font-size xl 24)
         (radius sm 4) (radius md 8) (radius lg 12))

       (component btn
         (interactive click)
         (padding (top (spacing sm)) (right (spacing md))
                  (bottom (spacing sm)) (left (spacing md)))
         (corner (radius sm))
         (bg (palette bg-surface))
         (color (palette text-primary))
         (children ..content))

       (component doc-item
         (interactive click)
         (padding (spacing sm))
         (corner (radius sm))
         (children ..content)
         (variant active
           (bg (palette accent))
           (color "#ffffff"))
         (variant inactive
           (bg (palette bg-surface))
           (color (palette text-muted))))

       (layout editor-shell
         (ui root
           (layout vertical)
           (width full)
           (height full)
           (bg (palette bg-primary))
           (children
             (ui header
               (layout horizontal)
               (width full)
               (height auto)
               (padding (spacing sm))
               (gap (spacing md))
               (align-y center)
               (bg (palette bg-dark))
               (children
                 ..header-content))
             (ui body
               (layout horizontal)
               (width full)
               (height grow)
               (gap (spacing md))
               (padding (spacing md))
               (variant narrow (layout vertical))
               (children
                 (ui sidebar
                   (layout vertical)
                   (width 1)
                   (height grow)
                   (bg (palette bg-surface))
                   (corner (radius md))
                   (padding (spacing md))
                   (gap (spacing sm))
                   (variant narrow (width full) (height auto))
                   (children ..sidebar))
                 (ui content
                   (layout vertical)
                   (width 3)
                   (height grow)
                   (bg (palette bg-surface))
                   (corner (radius md))
                   (padding (spacing md))
                   (scroll vertical)
                   (variant narrow (width full))
                   (children ..content)))))))

       (page editor
         (use-layout editor-shell)
         (fill header-content
           (ui btn-group (layout horizontal) (gap (spacing sm))
             (children
               (ui file-btn (use btn) (text "File"))
               (ui edit-btn (use btn) (text "Edit"))
               (ui spacer (width grow) (height 1))
               (ui settings-btn (use btn) (text "Settings")))))
         (fill sidebar
           (ui title (use doc-item (variant active)) (text "Chapter 1"))
           (ui title (use doc-item (variant inactive)) (text "Chapter 2"))
           (ui title (use doc-item (variant inactive)) (text "Chapter 3")))
         (fill content
           (text "Chapter 1: The Beginning" (size (font-size xl)) (bold))
           (text (|
             It was a dark and stormy night. The wind howled
             through the narrow streets, rattling shutters and
             sending stray papers spiraling into the gutter.
           |) (size (font-size base)))))

       (routes
         (route "/" editor)))
   |))
  )
 )

 (section convention-targets
  (doc "How the DSL maps to different rendering targets")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     The generator reads the (ui ...) spec and a target convention,
     then produces code. Each convention knows how to translate:

     ocaml-dream target:
       (layout vertical)  → div with flex-direction: column
       (width 1)         → flex: 1
       (bg (palette bg-primary)) → background-color: var(--bg-primary)
       (interactive click) → htmx attribute or onclick
       (variant wide)    → @media (min-width: 768px) { ... }
       (component ...)   → shared template include or sub-view
       (layout ...)      → layout template that pages extend
       (page ...)        → route handler that renders filled layout
       (routes ...)      → Dream router registration

     clay-c target:
       (layout vertical)  → CLAY_TOP_TO_BOTTOM
       (width 1)          → CLAY_SIZING_GROW(0)
       (bg "#e0d7d2")     → .backgroundColor = { 224, 215, 210, 255 }
       (interactive click) → Clay_OnHover callback
       (variant wide)     → conditional in layout loop
       (component ...)    → C function returning Clay tree
       (layout ...)       → C function wrapping page content
       (page ...)         → C function with page-specific content
       (routes ...)       → page switch function

     react target:
       (layout vertical)  → className with flex-col
       (width 1)          → style={{ flex: 1 }}
       (bg (palette bg-primary)) → style={{ backgroundColor: theme.bgPrimary }}
       (interactive click) → onClick prop
       (variant wide)     → useMediaQuery hook + conditional class
       (component ...)   → React component
       (layout ...)      → layout component with slot props
       (page ...)        → page component
       (routes ...)      → React Router config

     The convention also handles breakpoint selection: what screen
     width maps to "narrow" vs "wide", what the default layout is
     when no variant matches, etc.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section agentic-editing
  (doc "How agents edit UI specs using borge make")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     borge make

     1. Agent reads the current .borg files with (ui ...) sections
     2. Agent identifies planned sections or gaps
     3. Agent adds or modifies (component ...), (layout ...), (page ...)
     4. Agent validates: borge balance, borge check
     5. Agent commits changes

     This is NOT code generation. The spec IS the artifact. The
     agent is helping write the spec, just as it helps write any
     other .borg section.

     Drift detection: borge drift can check whether implementation
     matches the spec. A section marked (status implemented) whose
     corresponding code doesn't exist is code drift.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section future-work
  (doc "Possible extensibility for the UI DSL")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     - Animation/transition declarations
     - Grid layout beyond flexbox
     - Accessibility annotations: (aria label "...") or (role button)
     - Data binding: (text (bind model.field))
     - Route parameters: /user/:id
     - Nested routes and route guards (auth)
     - Redirects between pages
     - Form validation declarations
     - Input types and constraints
   |))
  )
 )
)
